aa50b6c7bc9953b5bb881acc970bf60f618be" "q-333671/selection/ma-v1/efc9608fe3ff99f7525e0771511c3d04c2cf428422907011e12db51b1e5f01b5/5a59af3f38b017545e7e42c871c2bce25643cc4ad0a7eeae33b1af38b48bbf9d" "q-333671/selection/ma-v1/efc9608fe3ff99f7525e0771511c3d04c2cf428422907011e12db51b1e5f01b5/903529158f6afa36a7706a80bf31d0af9580d50ddab64fa8f551fe3ace6efddb" "q-333671/selection/ma-v1/efc9608fe3ff99f7525e0771511c3d04c2cf428422907011e12db51b1e5f01b5/95f024a57ee28a313395a2da23e9b43d914154fcc533c839e2a4d25f91180173" "q-333671/selection/ma-v1/efc9608fe3ff99f7525e0771511c3d04c2cf428422907011e12db51b1e5f01b5/7955906577f1ba83855456a13c076c8cda0c958ffb3b4bcdcf1a4466dc67a33f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-333671"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+4y(t)-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}(t)-2(u(t)+1)+(v(t)+2)-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1-2u(t)-2+v(t)+2-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+4y(t)-7 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+4(v(t)+2)-7 \\\\  & =3u(t)+3-{v}^{2}(t)-4v(t)-4+4v(t)+8-7 \\\\  & =3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-333671/selection/ma-v1/efc9608fe3ff99f7525e0771511c3d04c2cf428422907011e12db51b1e5f01b5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-335485/selection/1139992a8a599c38933b766ec6287670302d189bffff712691e29dd9ed78f70d" :answer/id #uuid "5ef1febd-81e8-5e85-8ce9-5666189b95bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1}+5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335485/selection/e2eee26f816147f57c7a2d3d926b428d78c4eeda265ea99ea7ac4560738447fb" :answer/id #uuid "dcbbda0e-89da-50bd-9963-33a22970afd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}-‖{x}^{∗}‖" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335485/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "b8a0d2c1-8583-55b2-9a6e-89fc4adfb7f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335485/selection/87396e5f07dc408d8f713556ffe3ea80a7303f750fcdd63c6efa3b6817941670" :answer/id #uuid "6c0cc743-a71b-5074-95fe-e1a71aa572bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1}+{x}^{∗}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335485/selection/551dd1ea7e7bac6c6bd701b55450745ae020d94944976e90d8b3609af0e4d3aa" :answer/id #uuid "36d2c8e7-fb38-5710-9784-fb51a749512a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335485/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "75b67485-8ee0-5eac-85e2-6956e0322acd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335485/selection/551dd1ea7e7bac6c6bd701b55450745ae020d94944976e90d8b3609af0e4d3aa" :answer-field/choices ["q-335485/selection/1139992a8a599c38933b766ec6287670302d189bffff712691e29dd9ed78f70d" "q-335485/selection/e2eee26f816147f57c7a2d3d926b428d78c4eeda265ea99ea7ac4560738447fb" "q-335485/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-335485/selection/87396e5f07dc408d8f713556ffe3ea80a7303f750fcdd63c6efa3b6817941670" "q-335485/selection/551dd1ea7e7bac6c6bd701b55450745ae020d94944976e90d8b3609af0e4d3aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335485" :question/id #uuid "dbd0072c-9617-5dfd-8c07-1e1b8ec76bf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-335485" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=[-2,\\,4]^{T}.$ The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ After shifting using $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ the system becomes ${u}^{′}=Au.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be an eigenvalue of the matrix $A?$" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a linear system, the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors remain unchanged.\n\nThus, the matrix $A$ in the shifted system has the same eigenvalues as the system's matrix in the original coordinates. These eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ or any expressions that are equal to them.\n\nAmong the given options, the only quantity that is guaranteed to be an eigenvalue of $A$ is ${\\lambda }_{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335485/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335485"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333682/selection/4bdf1d5afa09155a3047d50204ed78c9e2823ad91ce490eab6941b2a4c83af23" :answer/id #uuid "e1e15ba5-d7f4-545e-990a-40d4cf85497f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{v}_{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333682/selection/eae587af1112838389f14efab6a973c0fab10684710b8bcf16edae43d7c8438c" :answer/id #uuid "5606a964-87de-5574-b471-d69242c7e2ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{∗}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333682/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "8f8459dc-570f-5a69-917c-239023a47cdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333682/selection/4bb94a77cfc4c5996cc2c4147f67c5394e164172a22a34bebe08ebd1ebf00223" :answer/id #uuid "4296219e-6e3d-5b72-9d74-55c5e1801055" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{v}_{2}-{x}^{∗}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333682/selection/4f6c680495c2600b3b913e4fabcff873fe2e1259160bd3d706962049d672fc99" :answer/id #uuid "a4c21628-6c25-50a4-aa86-2323e98849a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{v}_{1}+{x}^{∗}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333682/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f4e7885-dea3-5caa-be8f-f91f213b7a94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-333682/selection/4bdf1d5afa09155a3047d50204ed78c9e2823ad91ce490eab6941b2a4c83af23" :answer-field/choices ["q-333682/selection/4bdf1d5afa09155a3047d50204ed78c9e2823ad91ce490eab6941b2a4c83af23" "q-333682/selection/eae587af1112838389f14efab6a973c0fab10684710b8bcf16edae43d7c8438c" "q-333682/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-333682/selection/4bb94a77cfc4c5996cc2c4147f67c5394e164172a22a34bebe08ebd1ebf00223" "q-333682/selection/4f6c680495c2600b3b913e4fabcff873fe2e1259160bd3d706962049d672fc99"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333682" :question/id #uuid "331c7839-3f32-5d34-bd91-dc6188dfcb86" :question/math-academy-id "q-333682" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Two linearly independent eigenvectors of the system's matrix are ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ After shifting using $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ the system becomes ${u}^{′}=Au.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be an eigenvector of the matrix $A?$" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a linear system, the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors remain unchanged.\n\nThus, the matrix $A$ in the shifted system has the same eigenvectors as the system's matrix in the original coordinates. These eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ or any nonzero vectors that are parallel to them.\n\nAmong the given options, the only vector that is nonzero and is guaranteed to be parallel to either ${v}_{1}$ or ${v}_{2}$ is the vector $-{v}_{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333682/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333682"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-333683"] :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=2x-3$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfied the above conditions is $y=2x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-335522/selection/a6744dddb2e513ce8e9d215a219ebfbdbcf551240a91b9a6ce0603cd19a1ea23" :answer/id #uuid "e7d7e1d6-6eeb-5df8-a0c3-713c4026d553" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The shifted system has exactly one equilibrium point." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335522/selection/1008ff38e15813db49addc3189fe02a6b09e2c68891df60d1623f2b30f93bc4d" :answer/id #uuid "5893b10d-0693-59dc-b07d-aded06049c28" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The shifted system has exactly three equilibrium points." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335522/selection/b88ddc1c62ea9752ac2efbd9f83a155da953afc555b75e4863840043f4788cad" :answer/id #uuid "3a457e23-cbef-51f8-b844-325e516ebf5a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The shifted system has no equilibrium points." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335522/selection/72eb23bb0e77af69eb4018078cf59f07afeab2595e49d3cb5baebffc9d6fcd7e" :answer/id #uuid "f84c27dd-70a2-5194-9779-2ddab0978500" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The shifted system still has exactly two equilibrium points." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335522/selection/12c18327c0c90cd78305eaab741af5bc1d9129883ba869c5fb574a286c347910" :answer/id #uuid "7cf9d773-2597-5fae-af12-a252b19ab3cb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The shifted system has infinitely many equilibrium points." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335522/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7af3a157-9b35-531b-9c6b-a06be7623083" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335522/selection/72eb23bb0e77af69eb4018078cf59f07afeab2595e49d3cb5baebffc9d6fcd7e" :answer-field/choices ["q-335522/selection/a6744dddb2e513ce8e9d215a219ebfbdbcf551240a91b9a6ce0603cd19a1ea23" "q-335522/selection/1008ff38e15813db49addc3189fe02a6b09e2c68891df60d1623f2b30f93bc4d" "q-335522/selection/b88ddc1c62ea9752ac2efbd9f83a155da953afc555b75e4863840043f4788cad" "q-335522/selection/72eb23bb0e77af69eb4018078cf59f07afeab2595e49d3cb5baebffc9d6fcd7e" "q-335522/selection/12c18327c0c90cd78305eaab741af5bc1d9129883ba869c5fb574a286c347910"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335522" :question/id #uuid "f5f8ef0c-e30b-50dd-a0ab-1a422595b973" :question/math-academy-id "q-335522" :question/problem "A nonlinear system of ODEs has exactly two equilibrium points. The system is then shifted using the transformation $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ where ${x}^{∗}$ is one of the equilibria. Which of the following statements about the number of equilibria in the shifted system is guaranteed to be true?" :question/worked-solution "A shift of coordinates is a translation of the phase space and does not alter the structure of the system’s equilibrium set. Each equilibrium point of the original system is translated to a corresponding equilibrium in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, although one of the equilibria is moved to the origin, the total number of equilibrium points remains the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"The shifted system still has exactly two equilibrium points.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335522/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335522"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335516/selection/566b481c84349ae1e82c698bf3fd1de4b7aa1497ce26b3aed0742ee7d9fe05fd" :answer/id #uuid "70d95d0a-568d-58b6-a808-a631be1b12a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An unstable saddle point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335516/selection/eb8873dd24e72fbdcd7965053c0cef05c6d71c80ab3f3be35181c07ca8fb073f" :answer/id #uuid "33d11bfe-8c0e-53a6-bfa4-e507ca31c492" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A stable center" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335516/selection/96e057786dc42c9e8f0cea8bb816f2f023b176160c107c4dcf22a066651322ab" :answer/id #uuid "4cdb3b52-ea8a-5384-a8dd-76cb29898495" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A stable star node" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335516/selection/a79fb5cd435652b81fb9c834358b845f9ec3e0b337805c0222e10768b8867580" :answer/id #uuid "98c5756c-98ee-5bd7-a5a3-dfb973306080" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An unstable spiral source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335516/selection/5147da3c214ab47077bcdaf635b805a8b80a846b9899a56f4b34fe17a7a5078b" :answer/id #uuid "5242b2ad-aa36-55a6-ac51-4ec80c5c8167" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An asymptotically stable spiral sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335516/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3608012c-898f-503e-ab42-7599fb0d61b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335516/selection/96e057786dc42c9e8f0cea8bb816f2f023b176160c107c4dcf22a066651322ab" :answer-field/choices ["q-335516/selection/566b481c84349ae1e82c698bf3fd1de4b7aa1497ce26b3aed0742ee7d9fe05fd" "q-335516/selection/eb8873dd24e72fbdcd7965053c0cef05c6d71c80ab3f3be35181c07ca8fb073f" "q-335516/selection/96e057786dc42c9e8f0cea8bb816f2f023b176160c107c4dcf22a066651322ab" "q-335516/selection/a79fb5cd435652b81fb9c834358b845f9ec3e0b337805c0222e10768b8867580" "q-335516/selection/5147da3c214ab47077bcdaf635b805a8b80a846b9899a56f4b34fe17a7a5078b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335516" :question/id #uuid "91e2e185-1f83-5298-a5cd-e56df6733f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335516" :question/problem "A system of ODEs has a stable star node at ${x}^{∗}.$ The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following best describes the point $(0,0)$ for the shifted system?" :question/worked-solution "Shifting does not alter the local behavior near an equilibrium. So, the equilibrium retains its original classification (saddle, node, spiral, center, etc.).\n\nRecall that the shift moves the equilibrium to the origin.\n\nThus, the equilibrium at $(0,0)$ in the shifted system has the same classification as ${x}^{∗}$ in the original system.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"A stable star node\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335516/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335516"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21692"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-2y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t)-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, 2]^T.$ What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/2c994a3f48f4d9e12a179b03538977f72e033c5e539ec6f73c6d4529c1674299.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/d1aac4dbbbd1d7579a0a03c1440b3140efc18f43d6a8016e867e9419af693675.png)\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/6118cf06e6accf9f29665d66c4c4d00e0e7e2eb8bdbd98bd9044ee418422ca59.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/fb0d2631e167027b68263b62af5ba65d91442fdc89d1c16e39d6299f97835307.png)\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21693"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0, \\, -2]^T.$ What system is obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/951509e719a40caba7ecc5872d983b6cf83128d77af670c88846d2a06d329d8e.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/a3d02714f52ce42ec3e6d78d9cdd91f4c2ca14a59948f078d93318c94ec5e31a.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/cce8b4b07839490b3be0841f8a4e6e73b69426426504625cedecf738947b046d.png)\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t) = u(t), \\qquad\ny(t) = v(t) - 2, \\qquad\nx'(t) = u'(t), \\qquad\nv'(t) = y'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+(v(t)-2)+2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =5u(t)-(v(t)-2)^{2}+3(v(t)-2)+10 \\\\  & =5u(t)-({v}^{2}(t)-4v(t)+4)+3v(t)-6+10 \\\\  & =5u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+7v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=5u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+7v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21694"] :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=3x-1$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?\n\n- $y=3x+3$\n\n- $y=-x$\n\n- $y=3x$\n\n- $y=-3x$" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfied the above conditions is $y=3x.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
